# The likeliest place for a shift is "opposite". The general claim (S2-S17) is built mostly on relative comparatives such as greater/less and weaker/stronger, plus a few paired states such as heating/cooling. S19 treats life and death as opposite states, "as sleep is the opposite of waking". P1 and P3 both use the general claim in its general form, and P2 and P3 use life_death_opposites alike, so no single claim is used in two senses across its statements.
# A2 is weak because it needs "the dead" to be souls that go on existing below, while C2 only says the living come from what was dead. But "dead" does not occur in souls_below, so A2 joins two different claims rather than bridging one word between two senses.
